# Build shared UI from semantic tokens and clear component owners

Use this when several screens need the same visual or interaction contract. First find the existing token, style and component owners and their real consumers. Extend those owners; a new framework, component package or gallery is unnecessary unless the current structure has a demonstrated gap.

Dependencies flow from feature presentation toward shared foundations:

```text
page -> feature -> repeated composition -> accessible primitive -> tokens/styles
```

Tokens name roles such as surface, text, focus, spacing and motion. Foundation styles map those roles to the selected theme. Primitives own control semantics, focus, disabled/busy mechanics and geometry. Compositions combine recurring structure such as a labelled field or filter bar. Features own domain labels, allowed actions and data acquisition. A badge accepts a label and tone; it does not decide whether a record is eligible.

Promote a composition when actual consumers share its behavior, not just its appearance. Keep feature-specific variants local. An inventory can be a short owner/consumers/states/proof table in existing documentation; it need not become a registry. A primitive change needs checks at affected consumers, including keyboard use and narrow layouts.

## A small native-browser example

Save this as `controls.html` and open it in a browser. It has no framework or network dependency. The sample values are illustrative foundation choices, not a brand palette to adopt. Two feature sections consume one button contract. `actionButton({label, onAction})` owns mechanics; the caller decides what a refresh means. In an existing application use its canonical button and async owner instead of copying this implementation.

```html
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>Shared controls example</title>
<style>
  :root {
    color-scheme: light;
    --surface: #fff; --text: #18202b; --border: #536174;
    --focus: #124dc4; --space: .75rem; --radius: .375rem;
    --control-padding: .5rem var(--space); --focus-width: 3px;
    --cue-width: 1em; --status-height: 1.5em;
    --font: 1rem/1.5 system-ui;
  }
  [data-theme="dark"] {
    color-scheme: dark;
    --surface: #18202b; --text: #fff; --border: #aeb8c5;
    --focus: #9ebdff;
  }
  body { margin: var(--space); background: var(--surface);
    color: var(--text); font: var(--font); }
  section { margin-block: calc(2 * var(--space)); }
  .action { display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: var(--space);
    padding: var(--control-padding); border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius); background: var(--surface);
    color: var(--text); font: inherit; }
  .action:focus-visible { outline: var(--focus-width) solid var(--focus);
    outline-offset: var(--focus-width); }
  .action [data-cue] { inline-size: var(--cue-width); visibility: hidden; }
  .action[aria-busy="true"] [data-cue] { visibility: visible; }
  [role="status"] { min-block-size: var(--status-height); }
</style>
<main><h1>Shared controls</h1>
  <button id="theme" type="button">Toggle theme</button>
  <section id="reports"><h2>Reports</h2></section>
  <section id="activity"><h2>Activity</h2></section>
</main>
<script>
  function actionButton({ label, onAction }) {
    const button = document.createElement('button');
    button.type = 'button';
    button.className = 'action';
    const name = document.createElement('span');
    name.textContent = label;
    const cue = document.createElement('span');
    cue.dataset.cue = '';
    cue.setAttribute('aria-hidden', 'true');
    cue.textContent = '…';
    button.append(name, cue);
    let busy = false;
    button.addEventListener('click', async () => {
      if (busy) return;
      busy = true;
      button.setAttribute('aria-busy', 'true');
      button.setAttribute('aria-disabled', 'true');
      try { await onAction(); }
      finally {
        busy = false;
        button.setAttribute('aria-busy', 'false');
        button.setAttribute('aria-disabled', 'false');
      }
    });
    return button;
  }

  // A controlled scenario port: no timer, API, credentials or live mutation.
  // Only a dedicated demo/test consumer exposes completion controls.
  function mountExample(section, label) {
    section.dataset.starts = '0'; // test-only observation of actual action calls
    const status = document.createElement('p');
    status.setAttribute('role', 'status');
    status.setAttribute('aria-label', label + ' status');
    status.textContent = 'Ready';
    let settle;
    const action = actionButton({ label, onAction: () => {
      section.dataset.starts = String(Number(section.dataset.starts) + 1);
      status.textContent = 'Updating';
      return new Promise(resolve => { settle = resolve; });
    }});
    function outcome(label, message) {
      const control = document.createElement('button');
      control.type = 'button';
      control.textContent = label;
      control.addEventListener('click', () => {
        if (!settle) return;
        status.textContent = message;
        const finish = settle;
        settle = undefined;
        finish();
      });
      return control;
    }
    section.append(action, status,
      outcome('Complete ' + label, 'Updated'),
      outcome('Fail ' + label, 'Update failed; retry available'));
  }
  mountExample(document.querySelector('#reports'), 'Refresh reports');
  mountExample(document.querySelector('#activity'), 'Refresh activity');
  document.querySelector('#theme').addEventListener('click', () => {
    const root = document.documentElement;
    root.dataset.theme = root.dataset.theme === 'dark' ? 'light' : 'dark';
  });
</script>
</html>
```

The label and cue slot remain mounted. Busy state starts immediately; the static cue also works with reduced motion. `aria-disabled` keeps this busy button focusable, so the handler must guard every activation path. A native button turns Enter/Space activation into clicks. For permanently unavailable actions prefer the destination's existing native `disabled` contract and visible explanation. Do not hide the accessible name or infer authority from these attributes.

Check both consumers: focus the refresh button, activate it, attempt repeated activation, then complete or fail through the scenario controls. Confirm the busy name and button dimensions remain stable, one status owner reports each outcome, failure permits retry, and theme/narrow layout keep content readable. This tiny example does not implement authorization, backend failure classification, dialog focus management or a production data loader. The companion loading and browser-proof recommendations cover those different boundaries.

Use [CSS custom properties](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Guides/Cascading_variables/Using_custom_properties) for theme roles and read the [aria-disabled contract](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/Accessibility/ARIA/Reference/Attributes/aria-disabled) before retaining focus on an unavailable control. Existing framework props/slots can express the same responsibilities; Vue, React, CSS libraries, theme switching and particular pixel values remain project choices.

## Source basis and limits

These are inspected guidance, implementation/consumer and authored-test examples, not freshly executed source-project tests.

- StackOS@7b81c997330f4218941660ad8786827cc0d974df: docs/ui-design-system.md:18–44 and 92–125 (semantic roles and primitive contracts); ui/src/design/tokens.ts:1–7 explicitly identifies CSS variables and Tailwind aliases as value owners and itself as a mirror. The prose's shorthand that tokens are “defined” in TypeScript is not authority to introduce a second owner.
- SyncBooth@c4b1e9e3b7759cbd04e05dc5edd211f18b3d094d: docs/ui-design-system.md:20–51 and 102–104 (layer responsibilities); web/app/components/ui/SbStatusBadge.vue:4–55 and web/app/components/contacts/SendFactsDimensionPanel.vue:23–29 (neutral primitive plus real domain consumer). web/tests/e2e/button-busy-accessibility.spec.ts:4–55 authors accessible-name and geometry checks.

Source palettes, select policies, no-watch rules, gallery routes and framework restrictions are local choices. This recommendation imports the ownership and accessibility reasoning, not those choices.

# Keep compatible content stable while asynchronous work runs

Use this when refreshes or pagination replace a useful result with blank space, reset focus, or allow old responses to overwrite a new selection. Put the fix in the existing request/state owner and its presentation consumers. Do not add another cache, app-wide async engine or per-widget polling.

Retention is a predicate, not a universal rule: the displayed result must belong to the same record/project/query identity, remain permitted under the current authority, and survive the particular failure policy. A client generation check prevents stale presentation; the server still enforces authority. A scope key or authority revision is safe identity metadata, never a credential or authorization grant.

| Situation | Content and feedback |
| --- | --- |
| First load, no accepted result | Honest loading state; no fabricated zero, empty result or configuration conclusion |
| Accepted empty result | Explicit empty state for that successful query |
| Compatible refresh/page pending | Keep the accepted body and inspection mounted; show actual operation feedback in reserved space |
| Transient failure | Keep labelled last-known content only if the owner permits it; otherwise clear it and show the error |
| Identity/query changes, denial, revocation or invalid response | Clear incompatible content synchronously; reject late outcomes |
| Successful replacement | Commit a complete result once; preserve only still-compatible local interaction state |

Keep pending/error state per operation. Prefer the initiating control or one local status slot. Preserve the control's accessible name, focus and geometry; use one announcement owner. New real content can legitimately grow. Use the existing scroll/layout owner to preserve the reader's anchor rather than truncating values or freezing the entire page. Do not invent progress or delay a finished request for animation. Initial skeletons, cue delays and watcher policies follow the destination's selected design.

## A bounded state example

Save as `reader.mjs`. This dependency-free example illustrates one read boundary, not a replacement loader. Its caller supplies the complete compatibility key (including query and current authority revision), validates payloads before `accept`, and calls `invalidate` immediately on scope or access loss. `value === null` means no accepted value; an empty array is a real accepted result.

```javascript
export function createReader() {
  let generation = 0;
  let state = { scope: null, value: null, pending: false, error: null, stale: false };
  const snapshot = () => structuredClone(state);
  function invalidate() {
    generation += 1;
    state = { scope: null, value: null, pending: false, error: null, stale: false };
  }
  function begin(scope, { retainOnTransientFailure = false } = {}) {
    if (typeof scope !== 'string' || !scope) throw new TypeError('scope required');
    const value = state.scope === scope ? state.value : null;
    state = { scope, value, pending: true, error: null, stale: false };
    const ownGeneration = ++generation;
    let settled = false;
    const current = () => !settled && generation === ownGeneration;
    return {
      accept(value) {
        if (!current()) return false;
        if (value === null) throw new TypeError('null is reserved for no result');
        settled = true;
        state = { scope, value: structuredClone(value), pending: false,
          error: null, stale: false };
        return true;
      },
      fail(kind) {
        if (!current()) return false;
        if (!['transient', 'denied', 'invalid'].includes(kind)) {
          throw new TypeError('classify failure at the owning boundary');
        }
        settled = true;
        const retain = kind === 'transient' && retainOnTransientFailure;
        state = { scope, value: retain ? value : null, pending: false,
          error: kind, stale: retain && value !== null };
        return true;
      },
      cancel() {
        if (!current()) return false;
        settled = true;
        // Conservatively discard this cancelled read's displayed result.
        invalidate();
        return true;
      },
    };
  }
  return { begin, invalidate, snapshot };
}
```

Connect this idea to an actual loader by capturing its ticket before awaiting, then settling only through that ticket. Cancellation may ask the transport to abort, but stale-response rejection must still work if the transport ignores abort. Do not classify every exception as transient. Explicitly handle denial, expired session, not-found semantics, invalid payload and unknown failure under the destination's policy. A server authorization error can require clearing a wider protected session than this single-reader example.

Save the following beside it as `reader-check.mjs`; run `node reader-check.mjs` using a Node version with [structuredClone](https://nodejs.org/api/globals.html#structuredclonevalue-options) (Node 18+). Node is only the example runner; browser applications can use their existing test runner. The literal values and deliberate late settlements exercise the contract without clocks or sleeps.

```javascript
import assert from 'node:assert/strict';
import { createReader } from './reader.mjs';
const reader = createReader();
const a = 'project-A/query-open/authority-1';
reader.begin(a).accept(['first']);
const refresh = reader.begin(a, { retainOnTransientFailure: true });
assert.deepEqual(reader.snapshot().value, ['first']);
assert.equal(reader.snapshot().pending, true);
refresh.fail('transient');
assert.equal(reader.snapshot().stale, true);
const denied = reader.begin(a);
denied.fail('denied');
assert.equal(reader.snapshot().value, null);
assert.equal(denied.accept(['resurrected']), false);
const late = reader.begin(a);
const next = reader.begin('project-B/query-open/authority-2');
assert.equal(reader.snapshot().value, null);
assert.equal(late.accept(['wrong project']), false);
next.accept([]);
assert.deepEqual(reader.snapshot().value, []);
const cancelled = reader.begin('project-B/query-open/authority-2');
cancelled.cancel();
assert.equal(cancelled.accept(['late after cancel']), false);
assert.equal(reader.snapshot().value, null);
const old = reader.begin(a);
const latest = reader.begin(a);
assert.equal(old.fail('transient'), false);
latest.accept(['latest']);
reader.invalidate();
assert.equal(latest.accept(['late after logout']), false);
assert.equal(reader.snapshot().value, null);
```

This proves state settlement only. At a real consumer also exercise focus, draft/selection retention, reserved busy space, actual failure envelopes and scope changes through the real caller. A screenshot of the completed state cannot establish the pending transition or late-outcome behavior.

## Source basis and limits

- CFOBooth@6a663eb1ef4da35c1fd1a562722d64e85fd62794: docs/engineering/widget-loading-pattern.md:9–28 and 49–66 (selected state/geometry policy); apps/ui/app/composables/useConversationQueries.ts:105–186 (retained receipt, session fence and failure classification); apps/ui/app/features/conversations/queries.spec.ts:752–809 authors clearing-on-error, late scope response and denial scenarios. Inspected, not executed here. This project's selected policy clears financial receipts on ordinary error; it does not justify universal stale-on-error retention.
- StackOS@7b81c997330f4218941660ad8786827cc0d974df: ui/src/lib/stackos/projectRequestGate.ts:7–76 supplies scope and per-operation generations. It is an existing concurrency owner, not proof that every view already implements continuity. Task 816 is neither a prerequisite nor delivered evidence for this recommendation.

The generation sketch is illustrative and deliberately narrower than either application's request, authority and rendering contracts. Adapt it at the existing owner, with independent proof for the promised boundary.

# Reuse user actions and choose proof with independent oracles

Use this when browser specs duplicate interaction details or broad green suites hide an unexercised boundary. Reuse existing clients, fixtures and page/action objects; keep each scenario's expected behavior visible. An action helper describes user intent, owns locators and bounded synchronization, and returns observations. It must not quietly create domain data, retry mutations, or manufacture the expected answer.

Choose proof from the changed boundary:

| Obligation | Smallest relevant proof and its limit |
| --- | --- |
| Deterministic transformation/state settlement | Unit/contract check with literal or separately derived controls; no wiring or browser claim |
| Persistence, permissions or transport envelope | Real application/client plus database or loopback integration; test legitimate and denied calls and absence of unauthorized mutation where access changes |
| Control semantics, focus, layout or async presentation | Actual component in a browser, including pending/failure/recovery and affected consumer widths |
| Installed packaging or vendor behavior | Exact package/installed or authorized live boundary; a stub server does not prove a vendor |

These are complementary observations, not an obligation to run every layer for every edit. Honor the destination's mandatory gates, reuse evidence only while its candidate, dependencies and environment remain relevant, and widen when a failure exposes a real gap. Counts, source scans, snapshots and screenshots answer different questions. A guard can detect a forbidden import; it cannot prove keyboard behavior or transient geometry.

## A concrete browser scenario

The companion recommendation **Build shared UI from semantic tokens and clear component owners** supplies the complete `controls.html` example. Copy that file beside this spec. It mounts two real native button consumers with controlled completion/failure and no live effects. In an application, the same technique reuses the production presentation component through its existing narrow data/action port; the synthetic consumer supplies state. A public studio and a new dependency-injection framework are unnecessary.

This optional variant uses the project's existing [Playwright Test](https://playwright.dev/docs/writing-tests) runner and [auto-retrying assertions](https://playwright.dev/docs/test-assertions). Save as `controls.spec.ts` in a directory discovered by that runner. In an already configured project run its normal command, for example `npx playwright test controls.spec.ts`; preserve its browser installation, fixture and lifecycle owners. Do not add Playwright to a project merely for this pattern. Other native runners can express the same actions and literal observations.

```typescript
import { readFileSync } from 'node:fs';
import { expect, test, type Page } from '@playwright/test';

class RefreshActions {
  constructor(private page: Page, private label: string) {}
  button() { return this.page.getByRole('button', { name: this.label, exact: true }); }
  status() { return this.page.getByRole('status', { name: this.label + ' status' }); }
  async start() { await this.button().click(); }
  async settle(outcome: 'Complete' | 'Fail') {
    await this.page.getByRole('button', {
      name: outcome + ' ' + this.label, exact: true,
    }).click();
  }
  async bounds() { return this.button().boundingBox(); } // observation, no oracle
  async starts() {
    return this.button().evaluate(button => Number(button.closest('section')?.dataset.starts));
  }
}

test('pending, failure and retry keep the shared control usable', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.setContent(readFileSync(new URL('./controls.html', import.meta.url), 'utf8'));
  for (const label of ['Refresh reports', 'Refresh activity']) {
    const actions = new RefreshActions(page, label);
    await actions.button().focus();
    const before = await actions.bounds();
    await actions.button().press('Enter');
    await expect(actions.button()).toBeFocused();
    await expect(actions.button()).toHaveAccessibleName(label);
    await expect(actions.button()).toHaveAttribute('aria-busy', 'true');
    await expect(actions.status()).toHaveText('Updating');
    await actions.button().press('Space'); // must not start a second operation
    await expect.poll(() => actions.starts()).toBe(1);
    const pending = await actions.bounds();
    expect(before).not.toBeNull();
    expect(pending).not.toBeNull();
    expect(Math.abs(pending!.width - before!.width)).toBeLessThanOrEqual(1);
    expect(Math.abs(pending!.height - before!.height)).toBeLessThanOrEqual(1);
    await actions.settle('Fail');
    await expect(actions.status()).toHaveText('Update failed; retry available');
    await expect(actions.button()).toHaveAttribute('aria-busy', 'false');
    await actions.start();
    await actions.settle('Complete');
    await expect(actions.status()).toHaveText('Updated');
    await expect(actions.button()).toHaveAttribute('aria-disabled', 'false');
    await expect.poll(() => actions.starts()).toBe(2);
  }
});
```

The repeated-activation probe observes actual callback starts through the controlled consumer's test-only counter: one initial attempt, then two after retry. A presentation flag alone cannot establish that work was suppressed. The literal status text comes from the scenario contract, not a production formatter used to compute both actual and expected values. The bounds helper reports facts; the spec owns the allowed movement. Assertions inside an action helper may synchronize a popup becoming open, but domain expectations stay in the scenario.

This controlled test proves the sample presentation contract. It does not prove a real refresh request, backend authorization or network failure mapping. Add a separately owned real-client/browser scenario for those promises, with isolated data and exact cleanup. A route stub must be labelled as a substitute, and a request count needs an explicit observation window before it can support a no-unintended-request claim. Test both allowed and denied access through the actual caller when that boundary changes; inspect the real response envelope and persisted state, not merely a mock array.

Also walk the existing consumer at its actual width with keyboard input, long labels and selected themes; inspect focus, readability and pending-to-error-to-recovery transitions. Check reduced motion when motion changes. A completed screenshot cannot establish focus retention, absence of duplicate mutation or a late response after cancellation. The loading recommendation's generation checks cover state lifetime; this example covers the mounted control.

Record exact candidate/content, prerequisites, environment, command/scenario, observed result and cleanup. Distinguish authored/source-inspected examples, syntax/type checks, executed controlled-browser proof, real integration and installed/live proof. Retain failed observations and limitations rather than translating an inspected spec into a passing test.

